<div class="ephemera-page">

  <section class="ep-hero">
    <div class="ep-kicker">Classification · Quick reference</div>
    <h2>What kind of item is it?</h2>
    <p>Use standardized item types and codes so that materials can be consistently identified, inventoried, and retrieved.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Active item types</h2>
    <p>Use one of these seven active types when generating an ID and completing an inventory record.</p>

    <div class="ep-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">What it includes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Sticker</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">ST</span></td><td>Adhesive stickers, decals, and similar small-format promotional graphics.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Postcard</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">PC</span></td><td>Postcards, illustrated mailers, and similar postcard-format pieces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Poster</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">PO</span></td><td>Posters and larger-format promotional or illustrated display pieces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Minicomic</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">MC</span></td><td>Small-format comics, independently produced comics, and short comic publications.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Button/pin</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">BT</span></td><td>Buttons, pin-back badges, and similar wearable promotional objects.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Flyer</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">FL</span></td><td>Event announcements, handouts, promotional sheets, and informational flyers.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Other</strong></td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">OT</span></td><td>Related ephemera that does not clearly fit another active category.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Reserved types</h2>
    <p>These codes are reserved for potential future use. Do not independently activate them or use them in the generator unless the librarian has approved a change to the system.</p>

    <div class="ep-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Potential type</th><th scope="col">Reserved code</th><th scope="col">Possible application</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Zine</td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">ZN</span></td><td>Standalone zines and small independent publications.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Print</td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">PR</span></td><td>Art prints and related printed artwork.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bookmark</td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">BM</span></td><td>Bookmarks and reading-related promotional pieces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trading card</td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">TC</span></td><td>Collectible or promotional trading cards.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spare</td><td><span class="ep-inline-code">SP</span></td><td>Reserved system category; not a general-purpose alternative to Other.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>How to choose a type</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Classify by physical format</h3>
        <p>Choose the type that best describes the item's actual format, rather than its subject, creator, or intended audience.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Use Other when appropriate</h3>
        <p>If an item does not fit one of the active categories, use Other rather than inventing a new type or selecting a reserved code.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Keep similar items consistent</h3>
        <p>When you encounter several examples of the same format, classify them consistently. Ask the librarian about recurring ambiguities.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Don't overthink it</h3>
        <p>Routine classification should be quick. The inventory's keywords and title field provide additional ways to describe and discover an item.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Understanding the ID system</h2>
    <p>Each item receives a unique identifier made up of the type code, a collection-wide running number, and the generator's deterministic word component.</p>
    <div class="ep-callout">
      <strong>The essential rules:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Every item receives its own ID.</li>
        <li>All item types share one running number sequence.</li>
        <li>The number is never reused, even if an item is withdrawn.</li>
        <li>The complete ID must be copied exactly from the generator.</li>
        <li>The word component is generated consistently from the type and number.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p>Do not manually construct, modify, or abbreviate an ID. Use the <a href="https://clark.libguides.com/c.php?g=1565192&p=11748151">Ephemera ID Generator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>What if a new type seems necessary?</h2>
    <p>If you encounter a recurring format that does not fit the active categories, record it as Other and bring the question to the librarian. A new type should be added centrally so the generator, inventory, and staff documentation remain consistent.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="ep-footer">Clark College Libraries · Comics &amp; Graphic Novels Ephemera · Classification reference</div>
